      why_en: "The galactolipids are localised in the thylakoid membranes of chloroplasts and “ make up 70% to 80% of the total membrane lipids of a vascular plant, and are therefore probably the most abundant membrane lipids in the biosphere ” . The explanation offered is ecological and is stated as a hypothesis rather than a fact: “ Phosphate is often the limiting plant nutrient in soil, and perhaps the evolutionary pressure to conserve phosphate for more critical roles favored plants that made phosphate-free lipids ” (A p.349). The same logic covers the plant sulfolipids, in which a sulfonated glucose carries the negative charge that a phosphate would otherwise carry — the anionic head group is replaced too, still without spending phosphate. Czech 8-2-3 draws all three of these lipids and names them MGDG, DGDG and SL.",
