      options: [
        "Peptide B, because its higher pI means it binds the resin less strongly",
        "Peptide A, because a cation exchanger carries bound ANIONIC groups and therefore retards the net-positive peptide B more strongly",
        "They co-elute, because ion exchange separates on size rather than charge",
        "Peptide A, because peptides with a lower pI are always smaller"
      ],
      answer: 1,
      why_en: "The naming is the trap. A cation exchanger is so called because it exchanges cations, which means it carries bound ANIONIC (negative) groups and binds positively charged molecules. At neutral pH, peptide B (pI 7.8) is net positive and interacts strongly with the negative resin, so it is retarded; peptide A (pI 5.1, net negative) passes through and elutes first. On an ANION exchanger the answer reverses: A is retarded and B elutes first. The general rule that survives any version of the question: the protein whose charge is opposite to the resin's is the one that sticks, and whatever sticks comes off last. pI has no relation to size, so D is wrong on its premise.",
      why_cn: "陷阱在名称上。阳离子交换剂之所以叫这个名字，是因为它交换阳离子，这意味着它带的是共价结合的阴离子（负电）基团，并结合带正电的分子。在中性 pH 下，肽 B （ pI 7.8 ）净带正电，与带负电的树脂强烈作用，因而被阻滞；肽 A （ pI 5.1 ，净带负电）则直接通过，先被洗脱。在阴离子交换柱上答案反过来： A 被阻滞， B 先出来。无论题目怎么变都成立的通用规则是：电荷与树脂相反的那个蛋白会被粘住，而被粘住的最后出来。 pI 与大小没有关系，所以选项 D 的前提就是错的。"
