      why_en: "Rifampin inhibits bacterial RNA synthesis by preventing the promoter clearance step, binding near the active site of RNA polymerase and preventing extension of the RNA product beyond 2 to 3 nucleotides; it is an important antibiotic for tuberculosis, and resistance arises largely by mutation in its binding site (Asp516, His526, Ser531 of the beta subunit), with more than 600,000 resistant cases reported annually. Alpha-amanitin disrupts transcription in animal cells by blocking Pol II and, at higher concentrations, Pol III; neither Pol I nor bacterial RNA polymerase is sensitive — nor is A. phalloides's own Pol II — and because mitochondrial polymerases resemble bacterial ones and share that resistance, alpha-amanitin exposure reveals mRNAs arising only from mitochondrial transcription. Option 2 attributes actinomycin D's intercalation mechanism to rifampin and inverts alpha-amanitin's target. Option 3 contradicts the explicit statement that the mushroom's own Pol II is not sensitive. Option 4 swaps the two organisms entirely.",
